ask(c,'The diagram computes τ_ff from desired states. For plant M(q)q̈+h(q,q̇)=τ and E=q_d−q, which term generally remains on the right of M Ë+K_vĖ+K_pE?', ['M(q)q̈_d+h(q,q̇)−τ_ff','τ_ff−M(q)q̈_d−h(q,q̇)','Only K_pE, regardless of the model','Zero for every desired-state feedforward model'],['Substitute q̈=q̈_d−Ë into the plant and rearrange.','This reverses the residual sign for the stated error definition.','The proportional term is already on the left.','Desired-state and actual-state model loads need not coincide away from perfect tracking.'],difficulty='hard',kind='synthesis',formula=[FFE],pages=[730,734,735],steps=['Use M(q)(q̈_d−Ë)+h=τ_ff+K_pE+K_vĖ.','Move the error terms to one side.','Obtain M Ë+K_vĖ+K_pE=Mq̈_d+h−τ_ff.'],conflict=(735,'The feedforward scheme is assigned a homogeneous error equation without stating a residual condition.','The exact residual vanishes only when τ_ff=M(q)q̈_d+h(q,q̇), or under another condition that makes these torques equal. Desired-state precomputation alone does not ensure it.'))
ask(c,'Under the slide’s homogeneous-error approximation, why need diagonal K_p and K_v not decouple the joints?', ['M(q)⁻¹K_p and M(q)⁻¹K_v can still have off-diagonal entries','The inverse of every inertia matrix is necessarily diagonal','Diagonal gains always make the plant inertia identity','The desired acceleration must be nonzero at every instant'],['Multiplication by coupled inverse inertia can restore cross-joint terms.','A coupled positive-definite inertia generally has a coupled inverse.','Choosing gains does not redefine the physical inertia.','This structural coupling does not require nonzero desired acceleration.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[FFE],assumptions=['The residual Mq̈_d+h−τ_ff is zero or neglected exactly as stipulated; no general desired-state cancellation claim.'])
