c=concept(736,'Dual-rate scheduling','Slow nonlinear background computation and fast correction, with stale-model limitations.')
ask(c,'Which task-rate assignment is the proposed dual-rate implementation?', ['Slower nonlinear model computation and faster servo error correction','Faster nonlinear model computation and slower servo error correction','Identical slow rates for sensing, correction and every model computation','A fast model with no online feedback correction'],['The computationally expensive model is moved to a slower background process while servo feedback remains fast.','This reverses the intended split.','Two different task rates are the defining implementation idea.','Feedback is retained rather than removed.'])
ask(c,'A supplied dual-rate design runs feedback every 1 ms and the model every 10 ms. How many feedback updates occur per model period, and what is the model rate?', ['10 updates; 100 Hz','10 updates; 1000 Hz','100 updates; 10 Hz','1 update; 100 Hz'],['10/1=10 updates and 1/0.010=100 Hz.','1000 Hz is the servo rate.','Both ratio and reciprocal period are wrong.','The servo executes ten times during a model period.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Divide 10 ms by 1 ms to obtain 10.','Convert model period to 0.010 s and take its reciprocal:100 Hz.'],assumptions=['Periodic aligned schedules with negligible execution jitter; supplied example, not a mandated course rate.'])
ask(c,'What limitation remains if the arm moves appreciably between slow model updates?', ['The model values can become stale even while error feedback runs quickly','The model automatically becomes exact because it runs in the background','The fast loop can no longer calculate any position error','All nonlinear dynamics become constant by definition'],['Fast feedback does not force slowly refreshed configuration-dependent model values to stay current.','Background execution is a scheduling choice, not an accuracy guarantee.','It can still use current sensor values for feedback.','The physical configuration-dependent dynamics remain nonlinear.'],kind='scenario',difficulty='medium',pages=[732,736,737])
c=concept(737,'Dual-rate diagram pathways','Actual configuration inputs, highlighted background blocks and measured velocity path.')
ask(c,'Which blocks are literally inside the red background-process ellipse on the dual-rate slide?', ['B(θ)+C(θ) and G(θ)','M(θ), K_p and K_v','The arm and both error summing junctions','Only the desired-acceleration input'],['The ellipse encloses the additive B+C and G blocks.','M and the feedback gains are outside the ellipse.','Neither the arm nor the error junctions is enclosed.','The desired-acceleration path is well left of the ellipse.'],kind='diagram')
ask(c,'In that diagram, which measured signals are shown feeding the additive dynamic model?', ['Position θ and velocity θ̇ feed the B+C path, while θ feeds G','Only desired acceleration feeds both B+C and G','Only position error feeds G and only its integral feeds B+C','Actuator torque feeds G while desired velocity feeds M'],['The actual position line feeds model configuration inputs and actual velocity enters the B+C block.','Desired acceleration enters the command sum before M.','No integral-error path is shown in this computed-torque diagram.','These are not the drawn signal pathways.'],kind='diagram')
ask(c,'What can be concluded from the labels B(θ)+C(θ) on this page alone?', ['They identify an additive background-model path, but the page does not define separate B and C formulas','B and C are explicitly given as two constant scalar PID gains','B is explicitly identified as the inverse inertia matrix','C is explicitly identified as the integral of the position error'],['The figure labels their sum without defining the individual mathematical decomposition here.','They are model labels, not the separately drawn K_p and K_v gains.','The inertia block is M, and no such B definition is printed.','There is no integral-error definition for C.'],kind='exception')
