c=concept(743,'Lyapunov purpose and performance','Energy-like certificates without full ODE solutions; stability versus convergence and speed.')
ask(c,'What is the main advantage of the Lyapunov approach introduced here?', ['It can establish stability from an energy-like function without explicitly solving the motion equation','It requires an exact closed-form trajectory before stability can be discussed','It gives the unique optimal PID gains for any nonlinear robot','It removes the need to specify the system dynamics'],['The method studies a function along trajectories rather than first finding the trajectories themselves.','Avoiding that explicit solution is the stated motivation.','A stability certificate is not an automatic optimal-gain design.','The dynamics are needed to evaluate the derivative along motion.'])
ask(c,'A controller has a valid asymptotic-stability proof but converges slowly. Is that contradictory?', ['No; qualitative convergence does not by itself specify acceptable transient speed','Yes; asymptotic stability always guarantees the fastest possible response','Yes; any slow response is unstable by definition','No; because stability proofs never use the actual dynamics'],['The source distinguishes stability from performance; rate bounds require further analysis.','Optimal speed is not part of the definition.','A slow convergent response can be stable.','The derivative of the Lyapunov function is evaluated using the dynamics.'])
ask(c,'Which distinction qualifies the slide’s informal statement that Lyapunov analysis tells whether a system will settle?', ['Lyapunov stability alone means nearby states stay nearby; convergence needs stronger conditions','Lyapunov stability alone guarantees finite-time arrival at equilibrium','A nonincreasing candidate always gives exact settling time','A stable equilibrium requires energy to be strictly negative'],['Stability and attraction are different properties; later slides add derivative or invariant-set conditions for convergence.','Finite-time arrival is not implied.','A sign condition is not an exact timing formula.','The candidate is positive definite, not negative energy.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[743,745,747],conflict=(743,'The prose informally equates a stability conclusion with settling.','Use stability for remaining near equilibrium and asymptotic/invariance conditions for convergence; do not infer settling rate or finite arrival time.'))
c=concept(744,'Linear energy derivative and invariant rest','Kinetic/potential energy, signs, units, zero-dissipation states and boundary cases.')
LIN=fm('m ẍ+b ẋ+kx=0; v=½m ẋ²+½kx²; v̇=−b ẋ²',{'m':'mass','b':'viscous damping coefficient','k':'linear spring stiffness','x,ẋ,ẍ':'displacement, velocity and acceleration','v':'total mechanical energy','v̇':'energy rate'},{'m':'kg','b':'N·s/m','k':'N/m','x':'m','ẋ':'m/s','ẍ':'m/s²','v':'J','v̇':'W'},['Unforced linear mass-spring-damper; m,k,b>0 in the main stability example. An explicit boundary-case question overrides the stated k or b condition.'])
ask(c,'Which terms are kinetic and potential energy in the displayed candidate?', ['Kinetic: ½m ẋ²; spring potential: ½kx²','Kinetic: ½kx²; spring potential: ½m ẋ²','Kinetic: ½m x²; spring potential: ½k ẋ²','Kinetic: b ẋ²; spring potential: kx'],['Mass times speed squared gives kinetic energy; spring stiffness times displacement squared gives elastic energy.','This swaps the physical interpretations.','This interchanges displacement and velocity, breaking the energy units.','These are power and force-like quantities, not the two energy terms.'],kind='match',formula=[LIN])
ask(c,'Which differentiation and substitution correctly produce the energy derivative?', ['v̇=ẋ(m ẍ+kx)=−b ẋ²','v̇=m ẍ²+k ẋ²=−b ẋ²','v̇=x(m ẍ+kx)=−b x ẋ','v̇=ẋ(m ẍ−kx)=−b ẋ²'],['The chain rule gives m ẋẍ+kx ẋ; the equation of motion then supplies −b ẋ.','Differentiating a squared velocity does not yield squared acceleration.','The common factor is velocity, not displacement.','The potential-energy derivative has a positive kx factor, so changing its sign prevents cancellation.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[LIN],steps=['Differentiate ½m ẋ² to obtain m ẋẍ.','Differentiate ½kx² to obtain kx ẋ.','Factor ẋ and substitute m ẍ+kx=−b ẋ.'])
ask(c,'Let m=2 kg, b=3 N·s/m, k=8 N/m, x=0.5 m and ẋ=−1 m/s. What are v and v̇?', ['2 J and −3 W','2 J and +3 W','3 J and −3 W','2 J and −1.5 W'],['Kinetic energy is 1 J, potential energy is 1 J, and −b ẋ²=−3 W.','Damping removes energy regardless of the velocity sign.','The spring term includes the factor one-half and squared displacement.','This uses −b ẋx instead of −b ẋ².'],kind='numerical',formula=[LIN],steps=['½(2)(−1)²=1 J.','½(8)(0.5)²=1 J, so v=2 J.','v̇=−3(−1)²=−3 W.'])
ask(c,'For m,k,b>0, what does v̇=0 at an instant imply?', ['ẋ=0, but x may be nonzero and acceleration may restart motion','Both x and ẋ must already equal zero','The spring stiffness must be zero','The damping coefficient must be negative'],['At a turning point velocity vanishes while spring displacement and restoring acceleration can remain nonzero.','The derivative depends on velocity, not directly on displacement.','The question stipulates positive stiffness.','The question stipulates positive damping.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[LIN])
ask(c,'Why is the origin the only trajectory that can remain forever in v̇=0 for this unforced system with m,k,b>0?', ['Remaining there requires ẋ≡0 and ẍ≡0, so the equation gives kx=0 and hence x=0','v̇=0 at one instant automatically forces x=0 at that instant','A nonzero constant displacement produces no spring force','Positive damping makes the energy function negative'],['The invariant-set argument adds persistent zero velocity, not merely an instantaneous zero derivative.','A turning point is a counterexample to that instantaneous inference.','A positive linear spring produces nonzero force at nonzero displacement.','The energy remains nonnegative.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[LIN],pages=[744,747],steps=['Persistent v̇=0 requires ẋ identically zero.','A constant position also has ẍ=0.','The plant equation reduces to kx=0; k>0 gives x=0.'],assumptions=['For convergence, trajectories remain in a compact energy sublevel; the positive quadratic energy provides this for the linear system.'])
ask(c,'If b=0 but m,k>0, which conclusion follows for nonzero initial energy?', ['Energy is constant and the undamped oscillator need not converge to the origin','Energy strictly decreases because the spring is positive','All nonzero solutions become unstable solely because v̇=0','Every solution reaches the origin in finite time'],['With no dissipation, v̇=0 and oscillation can persist.','A spring exchanges kinetic and potential energy but does not dissipate it.','The positive conserved energy bounds the linear oscillator, although it does not establish attraction.','Conservation prevents nonzero energy from disappearing.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[LIN],assumptions=['This is the explicitly supplied undamped boundary case b=0, not the slide’s b>0 example.'])
ask(c,'If k=0 and m>0, why is v=½m ẋ² insufficient as a positive-definite certificate for the full state (x,ẋ)?', ['It vanishes for every state with ẋ=0, including nonzero x','It becomes negative for every negative displacement','It is positive only when velocity is exactly zero','It cannot be differentiated with respect to velocity'],['A positive-definite state function must vanish only at the origin, but displacement is absent here.','The squared velocity is nonnegative regardless of displacement.','It is positive for nonzero velocity, not zero velocity.','It is a differentiable quadratic.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[LIN],assumptions=['Explicit boundary case k=0; the candidate and full two-component state are stated, with no claim of a unique resting position.'])
issue(744,'semidefinite-energy','The linear example lists only b>0 explicitly and jumps from vdot≤0 to rest.','State m,k>0 for positive definiteness; use the zero-derivative invariant-set argument, not strict negativity in the full state.')
c=concept(745,'Direct-method conditions and domain','C1 positive definiteness, chain-rule derivative, local stability and global qualifications.')
LYAP=fm('Ẋ=f(X); v̇(X)=∇v(X)·f(X)',{'X':'state vector','f':'state-rate vector field','v':'scalar Lyapunov candidate','∇v':'gradient with respect to the state','v̇':'candidate derivative along trajectories'},{'X,v,∇v':'dimensionless in the supplied normalized examples','f,v̇':'per unit time; dimensionless when time is also normalized'},['Origin is an equilibrium f(0)=0; f is locally Lipschitz on a neighborhood and v is C1 there. v(0)=0 and v(X)>0 away from the origin. Derivative sign is checked throughout the neighborhood.'])
ask(c,'Which description correctly gives positive definiteness about the origin?', ['v(0)=0 and v(X)>0 for every nonzero X in the stated neighborhood','v(X)>0 including a strictly positive value at the origin','v(X)≥0 with unrestricted zero values at nonzero states','v(X)<0 for every nonzero X'],['The origin is the unique zero in the region under consideration.','A positive offset at the origin does not meet the stated normalized condition.','That is only nonnegativity/semidefiniteness, not strict positive definiteness.','This reverses the sign requirement.'],kind='definition')
ask(c,'For dimensionless X=(x₁,x₂), which candidate is positive definite on all of R²?', ['x₁²+x₂²','x₁²−x₂²','x₁²','1+x₁²+x₂²'],['The sum is zero only when both components vanish and positive otherwise.','It is negative when x₁=0 and x₂≠0.','It vanishes along the entire x₂-axis.','It does not satisfy v(0)=0.'],kind='scenario')
ask(c,'For an autonomous system Ẋ=f(X), how is the derivative of a C1 candidate v(X) evaluated along trajectories?', ['∇v(X)·f(X)','Only the partial derivative of v with respect to time, treating X as fixed','v(X) multiplied by f(X), without a gradient','The inverse of the Hessian of v'],['The multivariable chain rule contracts the gradient with the state-rate vector.','A time-independent candidate can still change because the state changes.','This is generally a vector and is not the chain rule.','No Hessian inversion is part of the stated derivative.'],formula=[LYAP])
ask(c,'Under the stated local Lyapunov assumptions, which derivative condition is sufficient for asymptotic stability via the strict direct-method test?', ['v̇(X)<0 at every nonzero state in the neighborhood','v̇(X)≤0 only at the initial state','v̇(X)>0 at every nonzero state','v̇(X)=0 on every trajectory with no additional argument'],['Strict negativity away from the equilibrium gives the asymptotic-stability criterion in the lecture.','A one-point sign test does not control the subsequent trajectory.','Increasing candidate values do not satisfy the stability certificate.','Zero derivative alone does not establish attraction.'],formula=[LYAP],assumptions=['Origin is an equilibrium, f is locally Lipschitz, v is C1 and positive definite, and the condition is valid in a neighborhood.'])
ask(c,'For the normalized scalar system ẋ=−x³ and candidate v=x²/2, what derivative and conclusion follow near the origin?', ['v̇=−x⁴, giving asymptotic stability by the strict test','v̇=−x², proving the exact exponential rate is one','v̇=+x⁴, giving the stated decreasing-energy certificate','v̇=0 for all x, proving finite-time convergence'],['The derivative is x ẋ=−x⁴, strictly negative whenever x≠0.','The exponent is four, and this certificate does not give the claimed exact exponential law.','The sign follows the negative vector field.','The derivative is nonzero away from zero; finite-time convergence is not established.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[LYAP],steps=['dv/dx=x.','Use v̇=(dv/dx)ẋ=x(−x³)=−x⁴.','The candidate is positive definite and its derivative strictly negative away from zero.'],assumptions=['x and time are normalized; the smooth scalar vector field has equilibrium x=0.'])
ask(c,'What additional feature commonly makes a nonincreasing Lyapunov function support a global boundedness conclusion, rather than only a local argument?', ['Compact sublevel sets containing the trajectory, for example from a radially unbounded candidate','Only that the candidate is positive at the initial state','Only that the derivative is negative at one time','Only that the candidate has a finite maximum over all states'],['A bounded sublevel set prevents the state escaping to arbitrarily large values while the candidate decreases.','Positivity at one state does not bound the set of reachable states.','A single derivative value is not a trajectory-wide certificate.','A globally bounded candidate may have unbounded sublevel sets and does not itself bound the state.'],difficulty='medium',conflict=(745,'The intuition says a decreasing positive function prevents the state growing without bound, without domain or properness conditions.','Local stability uses a suitable neighborhood; global boundedness needs an appropriate compact invariant sublevel or properness condition, not positivity alone.'))
issue(745,'theorem-scope','Informal direct-method statements omit equilibrium/domain/regularity and global boundedness conditions, and negative derivative must exclude the equilibrium.','Make local assumptions explicit, distinguish nonpositive from strictly negative derivative and qualify global claims by compact sublevels/properness.')
c=concept(746,'Nonlinear spring and damping functions','Sign-definite restoring/dissipative functions, integral potential and normalized examples.')
NON=fm('ẍ+b(ẋ)+k(x)=0; v=½ẋ²+∫₀ˣk(λ)dλ; v̇=−ẋ b(ẋ)',{'x,ẋ,ẍ':'normalized displacement, velocity and acceleration','b':'nonlinear damping function, not a constant coefficient','k':'nonlinear restoring function, not a constant coefficient','λ':'dummy displacement integration variable','v':'normalized energy-like candidate','v̇':'derivative along normalized time'},{'x,ẋ,ẍ,λ,b,k,v,v̇':'dimensionless under the explicitly normalized time/state convention'},['Normalized unit-mass model; b and k locally Lipschitz, b(0)=k(0)=0; ub(u)>0 for u≠0 and xk(x)>0 for x≠0.'])
ask(c,'How should k(x) and b(ẋ) be interpreted in the nonlinear example?', ['Functions of displacement and velocity, not necessarily constant coefficients times their arguments','Two constant gains whose parentheses indicate ordinary multiplication only','A position gain and an inertia matrix respectively','Functions of desired error only, independent of the actual state'],['The source explicitly calls them nonlinear spring and damping functions.','That interpretation would silently reduce the example to a linear special case.','No inertia matrix is assigned to b.','The arguments are the actual displacement and velocity.'],kind='definition')
ask(c,'Why is ∫₀ˣk(λ)dλ positive for negative x under the source condition xk(x)>0 away from zero?', ['k is negative over the negative interval, and the reversed integration limits supply another negative sign','The integrand must be positive even for negative λ','The integral is always negative for negative upper limits','The lower limit must be changed to x to force positivity'],['For λ<0 the sign condition gives k(λ)<0, and integrating from 0 down to x reverses the interval orientation.','The stated restoring-sign condition gives a negative integrand on negative arguments.','The orientation and integrand signs must both be included.','The source’s limits already give the correct positive potential.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[NON])
ask(c,'For the normalized restoring function k(x)=x+x³, which potential term belongs in v?', ['x²/2+x⁴/4','x²+x⁴','x²/2+x³/3','x+x³'],['Integrating λ+λ³ from 0 to x gives x²/2+x⁴/4.','This omits the integration divisors.','The cubic-force term integrates to a fourth power, not a third.','This is the force function itself, not its integral.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[NON],steps=['Integrate λ to obtain λ²/2.','Integrate λ³ to obtain λ⁴/4.','Evaluate at x and 0.'],assumptions=['All quantities are nondimensional; k is the supplied illustrative function.'])
ask(c,'Which pair satisfies the lecture’s strict sign conditions for every nonzero real argument?', ['b(u)=u³ and k(x)=x+x³','b(u)=−u³ and k(x)=x+x³','b(u)=u² and k(x)=x+x³','b(u)=u³ and k(x)=−x'],['u b(u)=u⁴>0 and xk(x)=x²+x⁴>0 away from zero.','The damping product is −u⁴<0.','The damping product u³ is negative for negative u.','The spring product is −x²<0.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[NON],assumptions=['u denotes normalized velocity and x normalized displacement.'])
ask(c,'For normalized x=1, ẋ=−2, k(x)=x+x³ and b(ẋ)=2ẋ+ẋ³, what are the candidate v and its derivative v̇?', ['2.75 and −24','2.75 and +24','3.5 and −24','2.75 and −12'],['v=½(4)+½(1)+¼(1)=2.75; b(−2)=−12 and −(−2)(−12)=−24.','Dissipative damping gives a negative energy rate for nonzero velocity.','The spring potential is an integral, not k(x) or half of it.','This uses b(−2) alone rather than −ẋb(ẋ).'],kind='numerical',difficulty='medium',formula=[NON],steps=['Potential is x²/2+x⁴/4=0.75.','Kinetic contribution is ẋ²/2=2, so v=2.75.','b(−2)=−4−8=−12.','v̇=−(−2)(−12)=−24.'],assumptions=['All state and time quantities are normalized; supplied polynomial example, not a printed numerical example.'])
ask(c,'Which condition is required to place the origin at equilibrium in the nonlinear model?', ['b(0)+k(0)=0; the usual stated construction takes each term zero there','Only b(1)=k(1)','Only that ẍ is zero at one arbitrary state','Only that the candidate is positive somewhere'],['At x=ẋ=0, the equation gives ẍ=−b(0)−k(0), which must vanish.','Values at argument one do not determine equilibrium at the origin.','An arbitrary zero acceleration need not give zero velocity or an equilibrium.','Candidate positivity somewhere does not define an equilibrium.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[NON],steps=['Evaluate the state equation at x=ẋ=0.','Require ẍ=0, hence b(0)+k(0)=0.'],assumptions=['The question isolates the equilibrium equation; the usual continuous sign-definite functions in the lecture’s construction have b(0)=k(0)=0.'])
c=concept(747,'Nonlinear dissipation and invariant-set completion','Chain-rule cancellation, instantaneous versus persistent rest, and nonlinear notation repair.')
ask(c,'Which sequence correctly derives the nonlinear candidate derivative?', ['Differentiate the kinetic and integral terms; factor ẋ; substitute ẍ+k(x)=−b(ẋ)','Set ẋ=0 for all trajectories; delete the spring term; declare v̇<0','Differentiate k(x) instead of its integral; square the acceleration; substitute b(0)','Replace k(x) by a constant before differentiating; ignore the upper integration limit'],['The chain rule and fundamental theorem give v̇=ẋ[ẍ+k(x)]=−ẋb(ẋ).','Velocity is not identically zero on arbitrary trajectories, and the final inequality would be non-strict there.','The derivative of the potential integral is k(x)ẋ, not k′(x) alone.','The nonlinear function and moving upper limit must be retained.'],kind='sequence',difficulty='medium',formula=[NON],pages=[746,747],steps=['Differentiate ½ẋ² to obtain ẋẍ.','Differentiate ∫₀ˣk(λ)dλ to obtain k(x)ẋ.','Factor ẋ and substitute the state equation.'])
ask(c,'At a nonzero turning point x≠0, ẋ=0 with b(0)=0 and xk(x)>0, which statement is correct?', ['v̇=0 at that instant but ẍ=−k(x)≠0, so the state cannot remain there','v̇=0 proves x=0 despite the stated nonzero displacement','ẍ=0 because all forces vanish whenever velocity is zero','The energy derivative must be positive because the spring is displaced'],['Zero velocity removes damping, not the nonzero restoring force.','This confuses an instantaneous zero derivative with the equilibrium state.','The spring force depends on displacement and remains nonzero.','The derivative −ẋb(ẋ) is zero at zero velocity.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[NON],pages=[746,747])
ask(c,'Which corrected statement captures the intended invariant-set argument on the continuation slide?', ['For trajectories that remain in v̇=0, ẋ≡0 implies ẍ≡0, so k(x)=0 and hence x=0','For every trajectory at every time, ẋ=0 by definition of stability','Every solution of ẍ=−kx is the identically zero solution','A single instant with ẍ=0 proves all future states are zero'],['Persistent zero dissipation, together with the state equation and strict restoring sign, leaves only the equilibrium.','Most stable trajectories still move while approaching equilibrium.','Even a linear undamped oscillator has nonzero solutions; remaining in the zero-velocity set is the missing condition.','One instantaneous acceleration value does not determine a constant trajectory.'],difficulty='medium',formula=[NON],pages=[746,747],assumptions=['b(0)=k(0)=0; strict restoring sign; consider trajectories remaining in the zero-derivative set, not arbitrary trajectories.'],conflict=(747,'The text says For all trajectories xdot=0 and then xddot=−kx has only x=0 as solution.','Restrict to trajectories remaining in the zero-dissipation set. Then velocity and acceleration are identically zero; retain nonlinear k(x), giving k(x)=0 and thus x=0.'))
ask(c,'In a normalized example k(x)=x+x³ and b(0)=0, what is the acceleration at x=−1 and ẋ=0?', ['+2','−2','+1','0'],['k(−1)=−2, so ẍ=−k(−1)=+2 toward the origin.','This omits the negative sign in the equation of motion.','This retains only the linear part of the nonlinear spring.','Zero velocity does not imply zero restoring acceleration.'],kind='numerical',formula=[NON],pages=[746,747],steps=['k(−1)=−1+(−1)³=−2.','b(0)=0.','ẍ=−b(0)−k(−1)=+2.'],assumptions=['Normalized state/time quantities and the supplied polynomial spring.'])
ask(c,'To turn the nonlinear zero-dissipation argument into convergence for a selected set of initial conditions, what must also be secured?', ['Well-defined trajectories remaining in a compact invariant set where the conditions hold','A positive derivative of the candidate outside equilibrium','An assumption that all trajectories start with zero velocity','An assumption that the nonlinear spring is secretly linear'],['Bounded invariant motion and appropriate regularity support the limiting invariant-set conclusion.','The argument uses nonincreasing, not increasing, candidate values.','Nonzero initial velocity is permitted.','The restoring sign condition works for nonlinear functions too.'],difficulty='medium',pages=[745,746,747],assumptions=['The vector field is locally Lipschitz; the candidate is C1; its sublevel set for the considered initial conditions is compact and stays in the domain.'])
issue(743,'stability-not-settling','Informal settling language can conflate Lyapunov stability with asymptotic convergence.','Separate remaining-near equilibrium, convergence and transient-rate properties in the question scope.')
issue(747,'nonlinear-invariant-set','The right-hand prose applies zero velocity to all trajectories and writes −kx despite the nonlinear k(x) example. The ODE xddot=−kx itself has nonzero solutions.','Interpret only trajectories remaining in zero dissipation; persistent zero velocity implies zero acceleration, then nonlinear k(x)=0 forces x=0 under the strict sign condition.')
